WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:08.360
On February 6th, 2012, Chinese police surrounded
the U.S. Consulate in Chengdu, China in a

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.420
tense, 30-hour standoff.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.790
Inside, Wang Lijun frantically pled for asylum
as he presented evidence of what would become

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:19.280
the nation’s biggest scandal in decades.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.470
As police chief of the country’s 3rd largest
city, Wang possessed documents, he said, which

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:30.439
could bring down his boss and one of the most
powerful men in China, Bo Xilai.

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:35.179
It all revolved around a British businessman
named Neil Heywood.

00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:40.030
In exchange for getting Bo’s son into a
prestigious boarding school, Heywood was rewarded

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.729
with political favors.

00:00:41.729 --> 00:00:47.469
But, for reasons that are still unclear, Bo’s
wife grew frustrated, and eventually poisoned

00:00:47.469 --> 00:00:48.929
the man.

00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:54.569
Dramatic, Oscar-worthy stories like this one
are a dime a dozen in China.

00:00:54.569 --> 00:01:00.589
In 2019, for example, this video circulated
on social media, showing 13 and a half tonnes

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:04.790
of gold hidden in a party secretary’s home.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:11.730
Between 2012 and 17 alone, 1.5 million officials
were disciplined for corruption.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:19.050
That’s nearly 700 criminal charges, demotions,
and dismissals every single day for six straight

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.500
years.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.220
It should be no surprise, then, that China
has, quote, “serious corruption problems”

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.350
according to Transparency International.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:31.380
But, here’s what is surprising…

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.220
Take each country and place it on a line from
most to least corrupt.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:40.820
Now, on the x-axis, show its economic growth.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.900
What emerges is the general trend you’d
expect: the less corruption, the stronger

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.190
an economy.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.200
Corruption, after all, is a tax on business.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:57.409
It scares away investors and diverts limited
resources toward unproductive roads to nowhere.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:06.100
…And yet, China is wayyy over here — with
both extremely high growth and corruption.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.990
How is that possible?

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:15.490
How has China defied predictions of imminent
economic collapse every single year for decades?

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.780
Not by eliminating corruption but by doing
what no other country has managed to do so

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.049
effectively: weaponizing it.

