00:02:54.299 --> 00:03:00.189
On the very first day of Finance 101, students
are taught about the “Principal-Agent Problem”.

00:03:00.189 --> 00:03:06.209
Capitalism, it goes, is all about specialization
— it isn’t efficient, or even possible

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:12.499
for each of us to sew our own clothes, solder
our own electronics, and be our own doctors.

00:03:12.499 --> 00:03:17.090
So we outsource each of these jobs to someone
more qualified.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:21.150
Modern life, in other words, requires delegation.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:26.290
But how do a company’s shareholders ensure
its managers, who they delegate decision-making

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:29.329
to, don’t misuse their money?

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:34.579
Replace ‘shareholders’ with voters and
‘managers’ with politicians, and you understand

00:03:34.579 --> 00:03:38.469
why eliminating corruption is a really hard
problem.

00:03:38.469 --> 00:03:41.219
That’s in a democracy.

00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:47.079
But even the most iron-fisted dictator can’t
possibly make, much less implement every last

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:48.760
decision on their own.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.870
They too must delegate.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:54.409
Say you’re the leader of China.

00:03:54.409 --> 00:03:59.669
Whether or not you have the people’s best
interest in mind, you also know your legitimacy,

00:03:59.669 --> 00:04:04.419
and therefore, your power, depends on some
level of competence.

00:04:04.419 --> 00:04:09.310
While nationalism and censorship afford the
Communist Party plenty of room for failure,

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:14.329
at the end of the day, your continued rule
requires food on the table, gas in the tank,

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:17.510
and at least some occasional clean water.

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:22.200
On a purely organizational level, take a second
to appreciate just how hard a coordination

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:28.660
problem it is to convert words, at the top,
into the actions, 99-layers below, which result

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:34.530
in more high speed rail than every other country
put together, and maintain 10% GDP growth

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.460
for years on end.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:43.660
Now, for the sake of simplicity, let’s condense
those (hypothetical) 99 layers into just three.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:49.080
At the very top of the pyramid are a tiny
number of elite party officials.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:54.750
Getting them to follow orders is relatively
easy — their next promotion depends on it.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:05:00.170
The problem lies between them and the public
— the ground-level civil servants who, on

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:05.650
any given day, actually deliver the functions
of ‘government’ — providing healthcare,

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.120
delivering education, and building infrastructure.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.960
Unlike the elites, these officials are usually
stationed in one city for their entire careers.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.480
They aren’t playing some “House of Cards”
game of political intrigue, they’re just

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.450
looking to put food on the table.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:26.710
And, hey, if they can convert what authority
they have into a new car or apartment, even

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:27.710
better!

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:30.720
…And therein lies the problem.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.070
The Chinese bureaucracy is huge.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:40.110
We’re talking 50 million party-state employees
— aka all of South Korea, or Texas and New

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.770
York put together.

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:47.150
That’s excluding its military — the world’s
largest, and State-Owned-Enterprises, which

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.840
comprise nine of the country’s ten largest
employers.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.290
Plus that number is from 2007.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:59.620
Meanwhile, thanks to outdated rules and widespread
evasion, only 2% of the population paid any

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.550
income tax in 2015, according to one former
official.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.430
In other words, there just isn’t enough
revenue to go around.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.310
Public servants are paid peanuts.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:19.020
The average nationwide monthly wage is $1,298
US dollars, yet the highest-paid civil servants

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.960
make just $824 and the lowest, a measly 80.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:31.330
This is a dangerous combination — lots of
local political power, yet, thanks to desperation,

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:35.960
every incentive to hinder and steal rather
than help the country.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.360
How do you convince these local officials,
who may not even make a living wage, to stop

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:45.830
harassing businesses, which you, the leader,
need to create the jobs and drive the economic

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:48.810
growth that keeps you in power?

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:53.280
Countries unable to answer this question end
up like the Philippines, paralyzed against

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.120
their will by an endless cycle of incompetence
and corruption.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:03.170
One obvious solution is to do as Singapore
does — pay them so much that they don’t

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.430
have to resort to bribery.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.790
Members of its parliament are given a monthly
allowance three times higher than the median

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:14.650
household income and its Prime Minister is
the world’s highest-paid political leader

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:18.790
— earning four times as much as the U.S.
president.

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:24.252
The results are compelling: no politician
has been charged with corruption in two decades,

00:07:24.252 --> 00:07:28.390
and the country is tied for second place on
the Corruption Perception Index.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:32.340
But, of course, this is easier said than done.

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.500
High public salaries are a luxury most of
the world simply can’t afford.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.350
It’s no coincidence that the countries at
the top of the list are also the world’s

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:43.350
wealthiest.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:50.770
Of the top 10, for example, the poorest is
New Zealand, with a per capita GDP of $41,000.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:55.370
China, on the other hand, discovered a much
cheaper solution.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:08:00.020
One invisible to the Corruption Perception
Index, which lums a half-dozen different things

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:03.900
together under this nebulous umbrella term.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:07.310
More interesting than how much ‘corruption’,
is what kind.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:12.930
Yuen Yuen Ang, in her book “China’s Gilded
Age”, argues there are actually four distinct

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:14.480
types.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:20.330
When the rich world hears the word ‘corruption’,
we imagine the 1% of the 1% — presidents,

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:25.560
prime ministers, and oligarchs — colluding
in poorly-lit rooms to siphon off public funds

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.100
for personal gain.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.180
Yachts, mansions, and private jets.

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:32.580
That’s “Grand Theft”.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:36.890
Conversely, people from lower-income regions
may not have to imagine.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:41.120
They can simply remember a cop soliciting
bribes.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.710
This is known as “Petty Theft”.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:48.650
Whether small or large — theft is bad for
everyone but the thief.

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:55.270
When your leader embezzles public funds to
buy rare leopard-skin hats, you receive nothing.

00:08:55.270 --> 00:09:00.400
But there’s another dimension: exchanges
— transactions in which the buyer receives

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:02.370
special treatment.

00:09:02.370 --> 00:09:07.250
If you need 20 permits to open a restaurant,
you almost have to pay what’s called “Speed

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:10.070
Money” to overcome the red tape.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:13.960
This is better than mere theft because at
least you get something in return.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:17.010
You’re not worse off after paying the bribe.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.050
Still, you’re certainly not celebrating.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:24.580
But then there’s something called “Access
Money” — exchanges that make both parties

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.430
much better off.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:33.580
Now, make no mistake: all corruption is ‘bad’,
but bad to who, when, and how?

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:38.300
If you’re a business, the theft kind is
just a loss — what economists would call

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.390
a tax.

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:44.170
Access money, on the other hand, is actually
an investment.

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:47.960
Suddenly you’re able to buy certain privileges
that you previously couldn’t.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:52.840
And this kind is precisely what China has
the most of.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:55.610
Here’s how it works:

00:09:55.610 --> 00:10:00.360
At the top of each regional government, remember,
is a powerful elite, whose performance is

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:04.610
evaluated based on objective metrics like
economic growth.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:05.710
The problem?

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:12.400
His or her next promotion requires the cooperation
of hundreds or thousands of poorly-paid subordinates.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:17.040
What motivation does the bureaucrat making
80 bucks a month have to play along?

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:19.480
So, the two reach an agreement.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:24.690
“Help me climb the ladder and I’ll make
sure you’re well taken care of”.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:28.690
Government departments are allowed to operate
almost like independent businesses, keeping

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:32.800
some portion of whatever they collect in fees
and taxes.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:37.060
Some of this money, in turn, is used to shower
workers with perks that would make even a

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:39.600
Google employee jealous.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:45.790
Everything from food, to vacations, parties,
housing, electricity, gas, child care, furniture,

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:47.050
and equipment.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:53.170
Although public wages are set below market
rates, formal salaries only comprise 24% of

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:56.830
the average bureaucrat’s total compensation.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:11:01.530
When these unofficial benefits are included,
you can see they’re actually doing quite

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:02.910
well.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:06.080
Bureaucrats may be short on cash, but that
doesn’t mean you won’t spot them on the

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.860
golf course or beach, sipping champagne.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.440
But don’t lose the forest for the trees.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.089
The point is not that government workers are
well-paid.

00:11:15.089 --> 00:11:19.500
It’s that they receive a kind of unofficial
commission.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:25.740
Every last cadre, no matter how low on the
org-chart, has a personal stake in the country’s

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.740
development.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:28.750
Does your office need new computers?

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:31.980
Is your family due for a tropical vacation?

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:37.540
Chinese bureaucrats can and do extort businesses
for a few quick bucks, but the big money is

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:42.500
in attracting businesses, from whom most taxes
are generated.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:48.800
Local governments ferociously compete to be
Tesla, IKEA, and Walmart’s best friend.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.680
One province, for instance, introduced “leave
business alone days”, during which inspections

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.410
and other regulatory “intrusions” were
banned.

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:02.370
Another county’s slogan is, quote, "Investors
are Gods…bureaucrats are humble servants,

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.850
and those who harm corporate interests are
sinners”.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:10.339
The genius of the Chinese system is that it
found an innovative way to harness corruption

00:12:10.339 --> 00:12:12.760
for the ‘greater good’.

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.750
Paradoxically, this incredible economic growth
was made possible by millions of officials

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:24.140
using public funds to play blackjack in Macau
and buy designer handbags in Paris.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:29.060
We think of the Chinese central government
as all-powerful and all-encompassing, able

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:32.090
to wield its authority far and wide.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:37.010
But there remain practical and physical limits
to what’s possible.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:41.140
Eliminating corruption is hard, if not impossible,
on a budget.

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:46.310
So, instead, China carefully manages which
kind is tolerated.

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:51.401
As you can clearly see, non-transactional
theft has been systematically targeted, while

00:12:51.401 --> 00:12:54.480
business-friendly favors are left alone.

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.010
Likewise, just as bureaucrats are managed
by an imperfect combination of carrot and

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:03.530
stick, so too are individual citizens.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:08.770
Chinese censorship is highly coercive, technologically
advanced, and effective.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:14.060
But it’s not perfect, and surprisingly,
nor is it absolute.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:18.970
Authoritarian governments like China’s are
a giant black box — shrouded in doubt and

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:20.220
secrecy.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:24.940
But understanding what it does and doesn’t
censor is a rare and revealing reflection

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:29.890
of how the Communist Party thinks and what
it fears most.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:33.820
This video has been about how China controls
its cadres.

